WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:00.893
JENNY BROWN: Hi.

00:00:00.893 --> 00:00:02.310
Today I'll be
talking to you about

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:05.190
an exciting new capability
Google Cloud has developed

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.290
called Apigee Adapter for Envoy.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.030
The goal of Apigee
Adapter for Envoy

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.030
is to help you integrate
Envoy-based services

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.320
into your Apigee environment.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.880
This means you could do things
like publish your services

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.190
as APIs, make them visible
to other developers,

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:33.000
add governance capabilities to
control access to those APIs,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.950
and analyze usage
metrics to see how

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.380
your APIs are being consumed.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.100
Let's talk a little bit
more about how we do this.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.950
Behind the scenes, Apigee
Adapter for Envoy boy

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.110
uses Envoy's external
authorization filter,

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:50.010
which is designed to allow
Envoy to delegate authorization

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:51.610
decisions to an external system.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.110
In this demo, I'll
show you how to use

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.170
Envoy to protect an end point.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:01.010
I'll add an Envoy
facade on it, which

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:04.040
is configured to talk to the
Apigee Adapter for Envoy.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.990
Let's dive in.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.470
I have an API product, which,
in Apigee, is the subscription

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.630
point or what
consumers subscribe to.

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:15.530
To begin with, I have nothing
configured in this API product.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:19.580
The first step is to bind
the endpoint httpbin.org

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.260
to the API product.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.780
With this command
I've just entered,

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.150
we have provided a
CLI, which allows

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.840
you to add the endpoint
httpbin.org to the product

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.720
httpbin.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.490
When I refresh my
page, you'll see

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:38.600
the endpoint is bound to
the API product, httpbin.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.270
Now we can turn our attention
to the components that are

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.370
going to run on my workstation.

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.700
I'll start with Analytics
which, is shipped by Apigee.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.590
It gathers metrics
from Envoy that

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.750
measures the
consumption of the API,

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.910
such as the number
of successes, who's

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.490
been invoking APIs, et cetera.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.110
I'm entering a
simple script that

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.560
starts the analytics
component of the adapter.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.565
And the analytics
component is now running.

00:02:04.565 --> 00:02:08.210
The next component I'm going
to start is Envoy itself.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:11.090
Before I start Envoy, I
want to highlight two pieces

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:12.380
in the configuration--

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.380
the prefix and how
it handles requests.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.440
I'll be matching
the prefix httpbin.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.600
I'm configuring it
such that all requests

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.310
will pass through
the Envoy proxy

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.320
and go to the Apigee adapter.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.110
The adapter will determine
whether the consumer has access

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.380
or not, and will reject
those that don't have access.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:33.140
Let's go ahead and start Envoy.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:37.010
So now I have the analytics
and Envoy components started.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.810
The last component
that I need to start

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.600
is the Apigee adapter
for Envoy itself.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.570
This is what advises Envoy
whether to allow or deny

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:45.500
a call.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.230
And for that, I've
got a script here.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.600
Now all three
components are running.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.400
Let's try and access
the API endpoint get.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.900
Because I don't have a
token, it didn't work

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.913
and the call was rejected.

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:01.580
Let me go and get the
token from Apigee.

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.870
Once I have the token,
I'll rerun the command

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:10.510
to access the API
endpoint, this time

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.370
by passing a token
as a parameter.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.240
And this time around, I'm able
to access the API endpoint.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.340
Now let's take a look at
what was in that token.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:28.010
It has the audience,
remote service client.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.510
It also indicates which products
the consumer can access.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:32.470
In this case.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:35.410
That's the product httpbin.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.860
These elements were
then enforced by Envoy.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.440
What I just showed you
is how an API consumer

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.180
would access this endpoint.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:44.560
Another way this
could be done is

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.250
by publishing this endpoint
and a product on an API portal.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.290
When developers come
to the API portal,

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:54.280
they can view and
access their APIs.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.550
The API portal also allows
developers to grab credentials

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.130
for their httpbin product.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:04.130
The credentials are masked
here for security purposes.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:05.860
Once developers get
their credentials,

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.920
they can access the API,
like I showed earlier.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.990
Through all these
interactions, analytics

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:12.700
are being collected
and transmitted

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:14.620
to the Apigee control panel.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.420
Looking at API
metrics here, you'll

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.450
notice that httpbin.org
is getting requests,

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:21.519
and I can view
the data for that.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:24.100
This is how you can use the
Apigee adapter for Envoy

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:28.660
to enforce policies for any
service that's using Envoy.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.160
Now that you know how it
works and what it does,

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.670
let me talk to you about how
to start using the Apigee

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:35.350
adapter for Envoy.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:37.097
The easiest way
to get started is

00:04:37.097 --> 00:04:39.430
from our Docs page, which
I've linked in the description

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:40.750
for this video.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.970
That's where we've outlined
three examples that you

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:44.890
can follow to get started--

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:48.250
native Envoy with Apigee
Edge for public cloud,

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:51.100
Istio side car
with Apigee hybrid,

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.340
and native Envoy with
Apigee for private cloud.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.470
The Apigee adapter
for Envoy can also

00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:00.880
be used by anyone who uses Istio
or Google Anthos service mesh.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.610
To get started with those, get
in touch with your Google Cloud

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:04.570
account team.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.940
In summary, the Apigee
adapter for Envoy

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.970
gives you an easy way to plug
API management capabilities,

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:13.510
such as publishing, visibility,
governance, and usage

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:16.660
analytics, into those
services that require them.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:19.210
Links to more information,
like code labs and blog posts,

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:20.680
are in the description below.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.602
And be sure to check
out our other videos

00:05:22.602 --> 00:05:25.060
and let us know what else you'd
like to learn about Apigee.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

